You are given the root of a binary tree with n nodes where each node in the tree has node.val coins.
There are n coins in total throughout the whole tree.

In one move, we may choose two adjacent nodes and move one coin from one node to another.
A move may be from parent to child, or from child to parent.

Return the minimum number of moves required to make every node have exactly one coin.



 int ans = 0;
    
    int helper(TreeNode* root){
        if(!root)return 0;
        
        int L = helper(root->left);
        int R = helper(root->right);
        
        ans+=abs(L) + abs(R);
        
        return root->val + L + R -1;
    }
    
    int distributeCoins(TreeNode* root) {
        // this will return 0.
      helper(root);
      
        return ans;
    }
    ***************************************************
    
    O(N)
    O(H)
    
    one call to helper(root) means it will return the value of the coins needed/excess by that node(subtree). 
    meaning if it is -ve that means that subtree requires some coins from it's root, and vice versa.
    
    also the ans is the abs of the excess of the left subtree and the right subtree. as this is the required move to move the coins from or to the parent.
